@sysname{} provides a complete implementation of the @commonlisp{}
package system as well as some parts of the Symbols dictionary.  What
sets @sysname{} apart from typical package systems contained in
@commonlisp{} implementations is that @sysname{} can also be used as a
library as well as a high level of customizability.

@node Usage Scenarios
@section Usage Scenarios

@cindex extrinsic
@cindex intrinsic
@sysname{} can be used as either an @termref{extrinsic} system in a
@commonlisp{} implementation that already has its own package system, or
as an @termref{intrinsic} system to provide a package system in a new
@commonlisp{} implementation.  For each of those cases, clients can
customize the behavior or select existing implementations of symbol and
package operators as well as the @link{Package System State
Storage,``storage'' of the package system} state.

@subheading Extrinsic Usage

@terml{extrinsic,Extrinsic} usage of @sysname{} allows it to exist
and function in a @commonlisp{} system that has its own native package
system.  One extrinsic use can be for testing @sysname{} without
affecting the native package system in the @commonlisp{} implementation
used for running the test.  Another use can be analyzing @commonlisp{}
code without creating the packages and symbols that occur in the
analyzed code in the ``host'' @commonlisp{} environment.

To load @sysname{} for an extrinsic usage scenario, the client should
load the @t{parcl-extrinsic} system.  Loading this system causes the
@t{parcl} package to be defined such that it shadows the names of
@commonlisp{} operators related to symbols and packages.  After that,
the core of @sysname{} is loaded as part of the @t{parcl-extrinsic}
system and attached to those shadowed names so that the symbol and
package operators provided by the ``host'' @commonlisp{} system are not
affected in any way.

@subheading Intrinsic Usage

@terml{intrinsic,Intrinsic} use of @sysname{} means that it is used
in a new @commonlisp{} system to provide a native package system when
the implementation does not have a package system of its own.

To load @sysname{} for an intrinsic usage scenario, the client should
load the @t{parcl-intrinsic} system.  Loading this system causes the
@t{parcl} package to be defined such that it uses the @t{common-lisp}
package without shadowing the names of @commonlisp{} operators related
to symbols and packages.  After that, the core of @sysname{} is loaded
as part of the @t{parcl-intrinsic} system and attached to the names
inherited from the @t{common-lisp} package so that @sysname{} provides
the definitions of the standard operators.

``Loading'' @sysname{} into a @commonlisp{} system that does not have a
package system is of course more than a little hand-wavy.  The client
has to make arrangements to resolve the associated bootstrapping issues.

@menu
* Package System State Storage::
@end menu

@node Package System State Storage
@subheading Package System State Storage

Another aspect of using @sysname{} that depends on the usage scenario is
the representation of the package system state: the mapping of package
names to package objects, the usage relations between packages, the
status of symbols with respect to packages, the home packages of symbols
and some other pieces of data.

@cindex traditional implementation
A @term{traditional} implementation is one in which the contents of a
package are stored in slots of an instance of the package object.
@sysname{} includes one implementation of this kind in the
@t{parcl-low-class} system.

@cindex trampoline implementation
A @term{trampoline} implementation is one in which the contents of a
package are stored in some kind of environment, and the accessor
functions on a package object trampoline to accessor functions on the
environment.  @sysname{} does not include an implementation of this kind
since any such implementation would be tied to a certain environment
implementation.

Note that both kinds of implementations are compatible with both
@termref{extrinsic} and @termref{intrinsic} usage.

@cindex native implementation
Another kind of implementation is a @term{native} implementation which
maps @sysname{} operators to the corresponding operators of the host
@commonlisp{} system.  This kind of implementation can be used only
@termrefl{extrinsic,extrinsically} and is useful mainly for tests.
@sysname{} includes a native implementation in the
@t{parcl-implementation-native} system.

@node Architecture
@section Architecture

As mentioned in @ref{Usage Scenarios}, clients may use @sysname{} for
several different purposes each of which requires a different
interaction between the client and @sysname{}.  To facilitate those
different ways of using the system, @sysname{} provides multiple
protocols which are tailored to specific purposes:

@itemize

@item
@cindex high module
The basic protocol consists of the @commonlisp{} package system
variables, condition types and operators.  In contrast to the other
provided protocols, this module includes macros as well, specifically
the @commonlisp{} macros related to symbols and packages.  This protocol
may be used in @termref{intrinsic} and @termref{extrinsic} scenarios.
Clients apply the ordinary @commonlisp{} package system operators and
don't care much about @sysname{}'s internals or, in an
@termref{intrinsic} scenario, even its existence.  We call the interface
provided to these clients (which basically consists of @commonlisp{}
symbol and package operators) the @term{high module}.  See @ref{High
Module} for the protocols provided by the module.

@item
@cindex client parameter
@cindex middle module
Some clients may wish to hook into package system functionality, for
example to monitor or modify the respective behavior.  Since the
@commonlisp{} package system operators are not customizable, @sysname{}
provides customizable generic functions with a @term{client parameter}
(see @ref{The Client Parameter}) which implement the behavior of package
system operations.  Each @commonlisp{} package system operator provided
by the @termref{high module} calls the corresponding generic function so
that clients can extend or change the behavior without any change to the
interface provided to clients which just use the @commonlisp{} package
system operators through the @termref{high module}.  We call the
interface for customizing package system operators the @term{middle
module}.  See @ref{Middle Module} for the protocols provided by the
module.

@item
@cindex low module
The goal of the third group of clients is to implement symbols and/or
packages with custom data structures without changing the higher-level
behavior of the package system.  One such client could be a
@commonlisp{} implementation that uses @sysname{}
@termrefl{intrinsic,intrinsically} as its implementation of the package
system.  Another client could need a simulated package system within an
existing @commonlisp{} implementation, for example for the purpose of
analyzing @commonlisp{} code or for sandboxing.  We call the interface
provided to these clients the @term{low module}.  See @ref{Low Module}
for the protocols provided by the module.

@end itemize

The following figure illustrates @sysname{}'s architecture visually in
terms of the above modules, the respectively provided protocols and the
relations between higher-level and lower-level functions:

