@node The Client Parameter
@section The Client Parameter

@cindex client parameter
As mentioned (@pxref{Architecture}), high-level protocols do not accept
a client argument while the lower-level protocols do.  As a consequence,
clients which use the high-level protocols have to communicate to
@sysname{} via some channel which client object should be used by the
underlying lower-level functions.  The fundamental mechanism for
providing this information is the variable
@varref{*client*,@toppackage{}}.  There are different strategies clients
can use to control the value of this variable.  @sysname{} does not
mandate any particular strategy as the most appropriate approach depends
on the usage scenario (@pxref{Usage Scenarios}).  The following
strategies might be useful to some clients:

@itemize

@item
One possibility for client code is to completely fix the client
parameter by defining its functions as @emph{closures}, closing over one
particular client object and either binding
@varref{*client*,@toppackage{}} to that object around calls to
high-level @sysname{} functions or supplying the object directly in
calls to lower-level @sysname{} functions:

@lisp
(let ((my-client (make-instance 'my-client)))

  (defun my-find-package (name)
    (let ((parcl:*client* my-client))
      (parcl:find-package name)))

  ;; Note: This is just an example. The function as written would not
  ;; be a suitable implementation of CL:PACKAGE-NAME because the
  ;; standard function accepts package designators.
  (defun my-package-name (package)
    (parcl.low:name my-client package)))
@end lisp

This strategy is probably most useful for either implementing a package
system that is not customizable in an @termref{intrinsic} usage scenario
or for defining a package system that is guaranteed to not interact with
any other package system in the Lisp system in an @termref{extrinsic}
scenario.

@item
Another possibility, which could possibly be suitable for some
@termref{intrinsic} usage scenarios, is to globally set
@varref{*client*,@toppackage{}} to a particular client object.  This
strategy establishes that client object as the global default but still
allows the behavior of package system functions to be modified locally
by binding @varref{*client*,@toppackage{}} around the function calls in
question.

@item
Another strategy, probably most suitable to ``local'' uses of the
package system such as employing a simulated package system for static
analysis of Lisp code, is to let the ``end user'' bind
@varref{*client*,@toppackage{}} as needed:

@lisp
(defun statically-analyze-lisp-code (source)
  (let* ((parcl:*client* (make-instance 'simulated-package-system))
         (cst (my-read source))
         (ast (parse cst)))
    #|...|#
    result))
@end lisp

The above usage scenario highlights a slight issue: assume the
@var{result} contains references to simulated symbols and packages.
When those objects are used in any capacity, even just being printed,
package system functions will likely be applied to the objects but
@varref{*client*,@toppackage{}} will no longer be bound to the correct
client object, or any client object.  As a result, using the simulated
symbol and package objects would likely signal an error.  One solution
would be to extend the scope of the binding of
@varref{*client*,@toppackage{}} but that would go against the idea of
using the package system only locally for the analysis.

To address this issue, @sysname{} provides a hopefully convenient but
slightly odd convention: if @varref{*client*,@toppackage{}} is bound to
or globally set to @t{nil}, the client object should somehow be obtained
from the symbol or package object to which the package system function
is applied.  This can be achieved, for example, by storing the client
object in the symbol or package object as demonstrated in the following
example:

@lisp
(defclass my-client () ())

(defclass my-symbol ()
  ((%client :initarg :client :reader %client)
   (%name :initarg :name :reader %name)
   (%package :initarg :package :reader %package))
  (:default-initargs
   :client (error "~S must be supplied" :client)))

;;; Ordinary implementation of the low-level protocol function
(defmethod parcl.low:symbol-name ((client my-client) (symbol my-symbol))
  (%name symbol))

;;; Alternative 1 for implementing the NIL-convention
(defmethod parcl.low:symbol-name ((client null) (symbol my-symbol))
  (parcl.low:symbol-name (%client symbol) symbol))

;;; Alternative 2 for implementing the NIL-convention
(defmethod parcl.low:symbol-name ((client null) (symbol my-symbol))
  (%name symbol))
@end lisp

The above symbol implementation would allow uses like this:
@lisp
(let* ((result (statically-analyze-lisp-code some-source))
       ;; At this point, PARCL:*CLIENT* is no longer bound for the
       ;; simulated package system.
       (symbols (extract-symbols result)))
  ;; Same here
  (print symbols))
@end lisp

@end itemize

@node Protocols for defpackage
@section Protocols for @t{defpackage}

Due to the desire of making things customizable and extensible, the
implementation of the @speclink{f,defpackage} macro in @sysname{} is a
bit complicated.  The implementation consists of the following aspects:

@table @b

@item Compile-time processing

At compile-time, the macroexansion of the @code{defpackage} form is
performed in the following steps:

@table @b

@item Macro syntax

@sysname{} parses the @code{defpackage} form according to the
s-expression syntax.  If the form contains syntax errors, continuable
errors are signaled.  The result of parsing is a collection of string
designators, package designators and symbol designators associated with
different @code{defpackage} options which serve as input for the
following checks and the expansion.

@item Early checks

Before the expansion starts, the supplied options are checked for
statically apparent errors such as symbols listed in both @code{:shadow}
and @code{:intern}.  For each such error, a continuable error is
signaled.

@item Expansion

The final expansion consists of a call to
@genref{ensure-package,@middlepackage{}} with the options and
designators as keyword arguments (possibly modified compared to the
original @code{defpackage} form due to error recovery).

@end table

@item Runtime processing

When evaluated at runtime, the produced expansion calls the generic
function @genref{ensure-package,@middlepackage{}} with the supplied
options.
TODO: where does the value for @code{*client*} come from?

@table @b

@item Designator resolution

@sysname{} resolves the designators for symbols and packages with which
@genref{ensure-package,@middlepackage{}} was called to symbol and
package objects.

@item Package lookup

@sysname{} calls @genref{find-package-using-package,@middlepackage{}} to
find an existing package with the specified name.  Regardless of whether
such a package exists, @sysname{} then calls the generic function
@genref{ensure-package-using-package,@middlepackage{}} to either create
a new package or update an existing package.  Two default methods on
@genref{ensure-package-using-package,@middlepackage{}} handle those two
cases: one method is specialized to @code{nil} and the other method is
not specialized so that it is applicable for any non-@code{nil} package
object.

@item Package creation or variance processing

The method on @genref{ensure-package-using-package,@middlepackage{}}
that is specialized to the existing package being @code{nil} calls
@t{make-package} to create a new package of the specified name and then
calls @genref{update-package,@middlepackage{}} to apply the remaining
options to that package.  Since the package is newly created, package
variance cannot occur.

The method on @genref{ensure-package-using-package,@middlepackage{}}
that handles updating an existing package first computes the differences
between the supplied options and the current state of the existing
package.  Aspects of the package for which no option has been supplied
remain unchanged.  For each package variance issue, that is a supplied
option demanding an incompatible change compared to the current state of
the package, @sysname{} calls the generic function
@genref{note-variance,@middlepackage{}} which allows clients to
customize the behavior in case of package variance.  Methods on
@genref{note-variance,@middlepackage{}} will typically either perform no
action or signal an error while establishing restarts that represent
different ways of resolving the variance issue.

@item Package update

Once the necessary changes for the new or existing package have been
computed, possibly after signaling and resolving variance issues,
@sysname{} calls the generic function
@genref{update-package,@middlepackage{}} to update the package object
and complete the process.

@end table

@end table
